>
  <p>Start a spring garden reset with a walk and a short list, not a trolley. New growth can sit beside winter damage, blocked drainage and plants that simply need more time.</p>
  <p>Inspect what is healthy, what has failed and what may become harder to manage in hotter weather. Then act on the causes you can identify and leave uncertain plant problems for observation or suitable advice.</p>

  <h2>Spring is not one garden season across Australia</h2>
  <p>Australia spans tropical, subtropical, desert, grassland and temperate climate zones, with local differences in rainfall, frost, humidity, wind and heat. A calendar-based task that suits a cool southern garden may be poorly timed in a tropical wet–dry cycle or an arid area approaching intense heat.</p>
  <p>Use current conditions as the trigger. Check local council water rules, state gardening resources and plant-specific requirements before pruning, feeding, planting or changing irrigation. The broader <a href="/guides/spring-home-maintenance-checklist/">Spring Home Maintenance Checklist</a> covers the building and outdoor structures; this guide stays with the garden itself.</p>

  <h2>Begin with a slow inspection</h2>
  <p>Walk the garden in daylight after recent conditions are understood. Mark healthy new shoots, bare stems, broken growth, weeds, unstable pots, erosion and low points where water remains. Look at leaves and stems before deciding a plant is dead: some species emerge late or naturally rest through part of the year.</p>
  <p>Trace damp patches uphill. A wet bed may reflect a leaking fitting, overspray, compacted soil, a blocked outlet or rainwater from another surface. If runoff appears connected to gutters or downpipes, move the building-side investigation to the <a href="/guides/gutter-maintenance-guide/">Gutter Maintenance Guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Check soil moisture, drainage and mulch</h2>
  <p>Push mulch aside and check moisture below the surface near the relevant root zone before watering. The Queensland Government notes that garden demand varies with climate, plant variety, soil, mulch, shade, wind and rainfall. A meter can provide another reading, but careful manual inspection and knowledge of the plant still matter.</p>
  <p>Do not confuse dry surface mulch with dry soil, or a wet patch with useful root-zone moisture. Water pooling, sour smells or persistently soft ground can indicate poor drainage or overwatering. Avoid digging deeply until underground services, irrigation and important roots are understood.</p>
  <p>Measure existing mulch rather than automatically adding a bag. Coarse mulch can reduce evaporation and weeds, but keep it clear of trunks and stems. It should not cover drainage openings, weep holes, paths or the base of structures.</p>

  <h2>Assess the lawn and every pot separately</h2>
  <p>Record thin patches, weeds, pooling, uneven irrigation and the way the grass is actually used. Do not turn this overview into a universal fertilising or herbicide program; the detailed <a href="/guides/lawn-care-basics/">Lawn Care Basics</a> explains grass, soil, mowing, water and treatment decisions.</p>
  <p>For pots, check that drainage holes are open and not sealed against a surface. Look for roots emerging from holes, a root ball that dries unusually quickly, leaning growth, cracked containers and top-heavy combinations that may become unstable. Consider whether dark or exposed pots will overheat as the sun moves higher.</p>
  <p>On balconies and courtyards, drainage, weight and walking clearance need extra planning. Use the <a href="/guides/balcony-and-courtyard-garden-setup-what-to-plan-before-buying-pots-and-planters/">balcony and courtyard garden guide</a> before expanding the collection.</p>

  <h2>Test watering equipment before hotter weather</h2>
  <p>Run each irrigation zone only long enough to observe it safely. Look for leaks, blocked outlets, spray hitting paths or walls, dry gaps and water running away before soaking in. Inspect hoses, fittings and timers for damage, then correct the schedule for current weather, local restrictions and actual soil moisture.</p>
  <p>A timer confirms when water was released, not whether roots received the right amount. Group observations by garden zone instead of giving every bed and pot the same routine.</p>

  <h2>Keep pruning decisions plant-specific</h2>
  <p>Remove only small material that is clearly safe and within the tool’s intended capacity. Pruning time, cut position and response vary by species, flowering cycle, climate and plant health; blanket spring pruning can remove flowers or expose stressed growth.</p>
  <p>Do not work from a ladder with cutting tools, use a chainsaw without appropriate competence and controls, or prune near powerlines. Energy Safe Victoria advises contacting the relevant electricity distributor, council or authorised specialist when vegetation is close to powerlines. Large limbs, structural defects and uncertain tree health belong with a qualified arborist.</p>

  <h2>Identify weeds and insects before treatment</h2>
  <p>Remove young weeds by a suitable mechanical method where practical, controlling seeds and protecting nearby roots. Identify unfamiliar plants before composting or spreading them, especially if a declared or environmental weed may be involved.</p>
  <p>Look for actual plant damage before treating insects. Agriculture Victoria notes that many invertebrates are non-pests, including predators and pollinators. Photograph an unfamiliar insect and check a reliable local identification resource. Avoid broad pesticide use simply because insects appeared with warmer weather.</p>

  <h2>Reset tools and store chemicals safely</h2>
  <p>Inspect hand tools for cracked handles, loose parts, corrosion and damaged cutting edges. Clean and store them using the <a href="/guides/garden-tool-care-and-seasonal-outdoor-reset/">garden tool care guide</a>, keeping sharp items stable and out of paths.</p>
  <p>Keep garden chemicals in their closed original containers and follow every label direction for use, storage and disposal. Do not create homemade mixtures or transfer products into drink or food containers. If the current storage point is unsafe, compare the access and weather checks in the <a href="/guides/outdoor-storage-ideas-australian-patios-balconies-gardens/">outdoor storage guide</a> before adding a cabinet.</p>

  <h2>Plan plants, shade and water before summer</h2>
  <p>Watch where afternoon sun, reflected heat and drying wind reach the garden. A pot may need a safer position; a vulnerable plant may need suitable shade; an irrigation plan may need separate zones. The <a href="/guides/outdoor-shade-setup-for-patios-and-backyards-what-to-check-before-buying/">outdoor shade guide</a> helps test coverage and wind exposure before equipment is added.</p>
  <p>Before buying a plant, confirm its local climate suitability, sunlight, soil and drainage needs, mature height and width, root behaviour, watering needs and ongoing maintenance. “Native” does not mean maintenance-free, and a plant suited to one Australian region may be wrong for another.</p>

  <h2>Finish with four priority lists</h2>
  <h3>Do now</h3>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>clear safe paths and accessible drainage points</li>
    <li>stabilise pots and repair simple hose leaks</li>
    <li>remove identified young weeds before they seed</li>
    <li>store sharp tools and labelled chemicals safely</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Monitor</h3>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>late growth, minor leaf damage and unfamiliar insects</li>
    <li>soil moisture, drainage and irrigation coverage after weather changes</li>
    <li>pots showing faster drying, emerging roots or increasing instability</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Plan before summer</h3>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>shade or relocation for heat-exposed plants</li>
    <li>watering zones based on plants, soil and local rules</li>
    <li>plant purchases checked against mature size and maintenance</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Call a professional if necessary</h3>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>trees near powerlines or large, unsafe branches</li>
    <li>persistent drainage, retaining-wall or irrigation faults</li>
    <li>suspected serious plant disease or regulated weeds and pests</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A useful spring reset leaves a shorter, evidence-based list. Protect access, understand moisture, prepare vulnerable areas for heat and buy only when a specific gap remains.</p>

